// The control API of a host FUSE mount of many stores (TASK-302): `fxdax-mount --control SOCKET
// --stores DIR MOUNTPOINT` serves one FUSE mount, whose root directory holds the stores attached
// to it, each at its name, however many: one OS mount, not one per store. Attach takes a branch
// of the mount's bucket, its lease with it, into a store at DIR/NAME, and shows its filesystem at
// MOUNTPOINT/NAME; Detach writes it back and lets its lease go, so that a VM, or another mount,
// may take the branch.
//
// It is served over a Unix socket, the mounting user's alone, mode 0600 whatever its umask, as
// every caller may do everything.
//
// Errors are gRPC statuses whose code follows the errno the mount failed with, which the
// `fxdax-errno` metadata entry carries, as a decimal number (Linux's), as the extent daemon's
// control API has them (fxdax/extentd/v1/extentd.proto): ENOENT is NOT_FOUND, EEXIST
// ALREADY_EXISTS, EACCES and EPERM PERMISSION_DENIED, EINVAL INVALID_ARGUMENT, ENOSPC
// RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED, ETIMEDOUT DEADLINE_EXCEEDED, EBUSY, ESTALE, ENOTSUP and EROFS
// FAILED_PRECONDITION, EIO and others INTERNAL.
//
// Calls block for as long as their work takes: an attach while its store recovers, a detach until
// the kernel lets the store go. A caller bounds them with deadlines; one that gives up does not
// stop the work, which completes, or fails, on its own.
//
// Compatibility: v1 only grows, as the extent daemon's does.

